// Implements [LEX-STATEMENT-BREAK] negative test.
//
// Default has no juxtaposition application and no statement terminator, so a
// statement ends where its line ends. Without that rule this exact program
// compiled and RAN: `let r = add 2 3` parsed as `let r = add` plus the orphan
// expression-statements `2` and `3`, so `r` bound the function itself and
// `r=[...]` printed a live code pointer to stdout — a different number on
// every run, between a truthful "start" and a truthful "end".
//
// Inside a block the orphan was absorbed by the trailing block value instead,
// so `{ let r = double 5 }` evaluated to the ARGUMENT 5 rather than applying
// `double`. Both are well-formed trees for source that means a call, which is
// why the boundary had to become a lexical decision rather than an accident of
// precedence.

fn add(a, b) = a + b

fn main() = {
    print("start")
    let r = add 2 3
    print("r=[${r}]")
    print("end")
}
